<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-inspection.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-inspection-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard-inspection.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">How appraisal works</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Process guide</p><h1>How Insurance Appraisal Works in Washington, Step by Step</h1><p class="lede">Seven steps from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what, and where claims most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Steps</dt><dd>7</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="Steps"><p class="toc-t">On this page</p><ol><li><a href="#step-1">Put the disagreement on paper</a></li><li><a href="#step-2">Send the written demand</a></li><li><a href="#step-3">Each side names an appraiser</a></li><li><a href="#step-4">The appraisers choose an umpire</a></li><li><a href="#step-5">The appraisers work the numbers</a></li><li><a href="#step-6">The umpire decides what’s left</a></li><li><a href="#step-7">The award is signed and paid</a></li></ol></nav><h2 id="step-1">Step 1: Put the disagreement on paper</h2><p>Appraisal starts with a claim the insurer has accepted at least in part. Before anything formal happens, write down what you think the estimate misses, attach your contractor’s figures and photos, and ask the adjuster to reconsider. Insurers in Washington are expected to respond to claim communications promptly under the state’s claims-handling rules, and many gaps close at this stage through a supplement.</p><h2 id="step-2">Step 2: Send the written demand</h2><p>If the gap remains, either side may demand appraisal in writing. A clear demand identifies the claim, says the parties disagree on the amount of loss, invokes the appraisal condition and names your appraiser or commits to naming one within the policy’s time frame. Send it in a way you can prove, such as email plus certified mail. Check first whether the policy requires a sworn proof of loss or other step.</p><h2 id="step-3">Step 3: Each side names an appraiser</h2><p>Each party picks a competent, independent appraiser, often within 20 days of the demand. Your appraiser inspects the property, studies the file and builds a line-item valuation. The insurer’s appraiser does the same. The two exchange estimates and start working through the differences.</p><h2 id="step-4">Step 4: The appraisers choose an umpire</h2><p>The two appraisers agree on a neutral umpire, ideally early so one is ready if needed. If they can’t agree within the time the policy allows, commonly 15 days, either side can ask a court of record in the county where the property is located to appoint one. In Washington, that is usually the superior court.</p><h2 id="step-5">Step 5: The appraisers work the numbers</h2><p>Most of the work happens here. The appraisers walk the property together, compare measurements and pricing, and agree on as many line items as they can. Many appraisals end at this stage with both appraisers signing an agreed award, and no umpire decision is needed.</p><h2 id="step-6">Step 6: The umpire decides what’s left</h2><p>Items the appraisers can’t settle go to the umpire. Each appraiser submits a position with supporting evidence. The umpire may inspect, ask questions or hold an informal meeting, then decides each disputed item.</p><h2 id="step-7">Step 7: The award is signed and paid</h2><p>An award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. A good award separates replacement cost, depreciation and actual cash value by coverage. The insurer then pays under the policy, less the deductible and earlier payments, with recoverable depreciation paid once repairs are done.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Who pays</b>Each side pays its own appraiser. The umpire’s fee is usually split equally. Ask any appraiser, including us, to put the fee arrangement in writing before you sign anything.</div>
<h2>What appraisal won’t do</h2>
<ul>
<li>It won’t decide whether the loss is covered.</li>
<li>It won’t award interest, extra-contractual damages or attorney fees, including under the Insurance Fair Conduct Act.</li>
<li>It won’t automatically pause your policy’s suit-limitation clock.</li>
<li>It won’t produce an amount above your policy limits.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where Washington-specific rules come in</h2>
<p>Washington regulates how insurers handle claims through the Office of the Insurance Commissioner and the claims-handling rules in chapter 284-30 of the Washington Administrative Code. The state also has a bad-faith statute, the Insurance Fair Conduct Act, that gives policyholders legal remedies in some situations. Those are legal matters for an attorney. Our role is the valuation.</p>
</article><aside class="side"><div class="side-in"><div class="side-box call-box"><h2 class="box-t">Talk it through, free</h2><p>Tell us what happened and what the insurer offered. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal fits.</p><a class="call-num" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-y full" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="box-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/how-insurance-appraisal-works/" aria-current="page">How appraisal works</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisal-faq/">Appraisal FAQ</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisal-glossary/">Claim glossary</a></li><li><a href="/claim-resources/">Claim resources</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div><section class="cta-strip"><div class="wrap cta-strip-in rv"><div><h2>Have a claim that doesn’t add up?</h2><p>Send us the insurer’s estimate. We’ll review it and tell you what we see.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-y" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
